00:12:01,050 --> 00:12:02,430
And along with the Public Interest

276
00:12:02,430 --> 00:12:03,900
Technology University network,

277
00:12:03,900 --> 00:12:07,020
we've been working on a qualitative research project

278
00:12:07,020 --> 00:12:08,310
to look at how students

279
00:12:08,310 --> 00:12:09,930
and young professionals view

280
00:12:09,930 --> 00:12:12,090
public interest technology careers.

281
00:12:12,090 --> 00:12:14,010
And I'm just gonna put a few slides up here

282
00:12:14,010 --> 00:12:17,460
so you can see some of the data as we go through this.

283
00:12:17,460 --> 00:12:22,440
So between just after the election in early January,

284
00:12:22,440 --> 00:12:24,210
before Trump was sworn in,

285
00:12:24,210 --> 00:12:25,590
we conducted a survey

286
00:12:25,590 --> 00:12:28,740
of almost a hundred students and young professionals

287
00:12:28,740 --> 00:12:31,140
and asked them a range of questions around

288
00:12:31,140 --> 00:12:33,510
how they're thinking about public interest technology

289
00:12:33,510 --> 00:12:35,370
careers in this moment.

290
00:12:35,370 --> 00:12:37,350
And what I'm gonna focus on today is,

291
00:12:37,350 --> 00:12:38,340
is the really broad

292
00:12:38,340 --> 00:12:40,500
and expansive vision that the students

293
00:12:40,500 --> 00:12:42,300
and young professionals shared with us

294
00:12:42,300 --> 00:12:44,970
for what this career path can mean to them,

295
00:12:44,970 --> 00:12:48,543
even in the face of potentially changing circumstances.

296
00:12:50,910 --> 00:12:53,790
So we first asked a question about what type

297
00:12:53,790 --> 00:12:56,940
of public interest issues are you interested in?

298
00:12:56,940 --> 00:12:58,950
And what the data shows us here

299
00:12:58,950 --> 00:13:01,320
is that the students want career paths

300
00:13:01,320 --> 00:13:04,560
that are justice oriented, that are intersectional,

301
00:13:04,560 --> 00:13:06,300
and that really speak to their personal

302
00:13:06,300 --> 00:13:08,580
circumstances and ideals.

303
00:13:08,580 --> 00:13:10,830
And so what we see is they're interested

304
00:13:10,830 --> 00:13:13,110
in a really broad variety of subjects,

305
00:13:13,110 --> 00:13:16,500
from core equity domains like education,

306
00:13:16,500 --> 00:13:19,530
economic justice, human rights, healthcare,

307
00:13:19,530 --> 00:13:22,200
racial justice, and more.

308
00:13:22,200 --> 00:13:24,420
They're interested in issues that come,

309
00:13:24,420 --> 00:13:25,770
that are more policy heavy,

310
00:13:25,770 --> 00:13:27,990
like healthcare, voting rights,

311
00:13:27,990 --> 00:13:32,370
immigration, data privacy, and environmental justice.

312
00:13:32,370 --> 00:13:34,710
They also have very community specific

313
00:13:34,710 --> 00:13:36,450
or community centric concerns,

314
00:13:36,450 --> 00:13:38,250
such as tribal sovereignty,

315
00:13:38,250 --> 00:13:41,580
veterans benefits, and mobility exclusion.

316
00:13:41,580 --> 00:13:43,410
And they're really interested in digital rights

317
00:13:43,410 --> 00:13:45,540
and platform governance, including, of course,

318
00:13:45,540 --> 00:13:46,920
algorithmic accountability,

319
00:13:46,920 --> 00:13:49,410
surveillance and AI ethics.

320
00:13:49,410 --> 00:13:51,210
And so what this tells us,

321
00:13:51,210 --> 00:13:52,860
or one insight we can draw from this,

322
00:13:52,860 --> 00:13:55,770
is that students are really not just seeking

323
00:13:55,770 --> 00:13:57,720
to apply tech to policy,

324
00:13:57,720 --> 00:14:00,000
but they want to use public interest technology

325
00:14:00,000 --> 00:14:02,340
to address structural inequalities

326
00:14:02,340 --> 00:14:04,590
and to repair historic harms

327
00:14:04,590 --> 00:14:06,060
through their participation

328
00:14:06,060 --> 00:14:08,280
in public interest technology careers

329
00:14:08,280 --> 00:14:10,023
across different sectors.

330
00:14:11,820 --> 00:14:15,840
Second, we ask them what type of work they're interested in.

331
00:14:15,840 --> 00:14:17,280
And again, we found

332
00:14:17,280 --> 00:14:18,390
that students are interested

333
00:14:18,390 --> 00:14:21,420
in a really wide array of types of work

334
00:14:21,420 --> 00:14:23,850
that really extend far beyond the reach

335
00:14:23,850 --> 00:14:25,410
of traditional tech roles

336
00:14:25,410 --> 00:14:28,560
or what we might interpret as traditional tech roles.

337
00:14:28,560 --> 00:14:31,620
So, students are interested in research and analysis work,

338
00:14:31,620 --> 00:14:35,490
from data science to investigations to policy research.

339
00:14:35,490 --> 00:14:39,570
They're interested in advocacy and policy domains.

340
00:14:39,570 --> 00:14:43,260
They're interested in community engagement and education,

341
00:14:43,260 --> 00:14:46,020
including organizing and teaching.

342
00:14:46,020 --> 00:14:47,250
They're interested, of course,

343
00:14:47,250 --> 00:14:49,140
in the more traditional tech field

344
00:14:49,140 --> 00:14:51,060
of product development and design,

345
00:14:51,060 --> 00:14:54,240
including user experience, service design,

346
00:14:54,240 --> 00:14:56,460
and ethical tech development.

347
00:14:56,460 --> 00:14:59,520
And then they're also interested in more legal

348
00:14:59,520 --> 00:15:02,040
and journalistic storytelling type work

349
00:15:02,040 --> 00:15:04,530
that might involve civil rights litigation

350
00:15:04,530 --> 00:15:06,300
or investigative reporting.

351
00:15:06,300 --> 00:15:07,950
And so again, this is confirming

352
00:15:07,950 --> 00:15:09,660
that students really see technology,

353
00:15:09,660 --> 00:15:11,940
not as a standalone skillset,

354
00:15:11,940 --> 00:15:15,000
but one that is going to be embedded in broader civil

355
00:15:15,000 --> 00:15:16,350
and political work.

356
00:15:16,350 --> 00:15:19,350
And they're really drawn to interdisciplinary careers

357
00:15:19,350 --> 00:15:21,960
that blend ethics with storytelling,

358
00:15:21,960 --> 00:15:24,933
with community knowledge, with systems thinking, and more.

359
00:15:26,370 --> 00:15:28,380
Finally, we ask them what types

360
00:15:28,380 --> 00:15:31,320
of organizations they're interested in working for.

361
00:15:31,320 --> 00:15:32,820
And while there is,

362
00:15:32,820 --> 00:15:37,820
of course, a strong interest in nonprofit government work,

363
00:15:38,010 --> 00:15:41,220
a surprising number of students in the public interest space

364
00:15:41,220 --> 00:15:45,390
also expressed interest in working at technology companies.

365
00:15:45,390 --> 00:15:48,420
And I think this probably reflects a pragmatic response

366
00:15:48,420 --> 00:15:50,267
to compensation realities.

367
00:15:50,267 --> 00:15:53,610
You know, the private sector versus the public sector,

368
00:15:53,610 --> 00:15:55,950
but also a fairly strategic mindset.

369
00:15:55,950 --> 00:15:58,380
I think students today are thinking about ways

370
00:15:58,380 --> 00:16:01,440
they can reshape technology from the inside

371
00:16:01,440 --> 00:16:02,820
and they want to bring ethics

372
00:16:02,820 --> 00:16:06,960
and equity into those dominant tech institutions.

373
00:16:06,960 --> 00:16:09,720
And of course others are drawn to

374
00:16:09,720 --> 00:16:12,060
where we might assume public interest students

375
00:16:12,060 --> 00:16:15,360
are drawn to, like government, nonprofit, and think tanks.

376
00:16:15,360 --> 00:16:16,860
But increasingly, students are thinking

377
00:16:16,860 --> 00:16:19,140
about academia as well as a home

378
00:16:19,140 --> 00:16:22,710
for public interest technology career paths.

379
00:16:22,710 --> 00:16:25,290
So why does this matter?

380
00:16:25,290 --> 00:16:27,030
I think it matters in this moment,

381
00:16:27,030 --> 00:16:28,800
because public interest technology

382
00:16:28,800 --> 00:16:30,960
is increasingly under siege

383
00:16:30,960 --> 00:16:34,110
due to some changing political circumstances,

384
00:16:34,110 --> 00:16:36,750
a lack of investment overall,

385
00:16:36,750 --> 00:16:38,610
not just in this current moment,

386
00:16:38,610 --> 00:16:41,850
and a lack of education, training, and career pathways

387
00:16:41,850 --> 00:16:44,850
that are legible to our students.

388
00:16:44,850 --> 00:16:47,040
But at the same time, the research is telling us

389
00:16:47,040 --> 00:16:49,200
that young people want to do this work.

390
00:16:49,200 --> 00:16:52,710
And so it's really incumbent upon us as educators

391
00:16:52,710 --> 00:16:57,300
to partner with funders, with industry, with civil society,

392
00:16:57,300 --> 00:16:58,470
and with government,

393
00:16:58,470 --> 00:17:02,010
to begin to create those viable and visible pathways,

394
00:17:02,010 --> 00:17:03,000
so that our students

395
00:17:03,000 --> 00:17:05,160
and young professionals can pursue these careers

396
00:17:05,160 --> 00:17:07,860
that they are already so passionate about.

397
00:17:07,860 --> 00:17:09,510
And I'll leave it there and maybe come back

398
00:17:09,510 --> 00:17:12,150
to some of this data a little bit later.

399
00:17:12,150 --> 00:17:13,590
- I'm sure there will be many questions

400
00:17:13,590 --> 00:17:14,490
about this rich data.

401
00:17:14,490 --> 00:17:16,410
Thank you so much for taking the initiative

402
00:17:16,410 --> 00:17:20,613
to dive in on this, especially at this time.

403
00:17:21,450 --> 00:17:23,820
My next question is for Meredith.

404
00:17:23,820 --> 00:17:27,990
I'm curious if you see any of this reflected in the interest

405
00:17:27,990 --> 00:17:29,910
of your students at UC, Berkeley,

406
00:17:29,910 --> 00:17:33,540
how you've then aligned academic pathways

407
00:17:33,540 --> 00:17:36,030
across institutions.

408
00:17:36,030 --> 00:17:38,820
Has this shifted academic priorities

409
00:17:38,820 --> 00:17:43,620
and also just sort of the multidisciplinary aspect of this.

410
00:17:43,620 --> 00:17:47,153
How has that kind of bled into your approach?

411
00:17:47,153 --> 00:17:48,990
- Absolutely.

412
00:17:48,990 --> 00:17:50,460
- I'll mix it up, but I'll try this one.

413
00:17:50,460 --> 00:17:51,293
- Okay.

414
00:17:52,710 --> 00:17:55,740
- Really appreciate the question.

415
00:17:55,740 --> 00:17:58,248
At UC, Berkeley,

416
00:17:58,248 --> 00:18:00,990
our new College of Computing, Data Science, and Society

417
00:18:00,990 --> 00:18:03,570
includes the data science major,

418
00:18:03,570 --> 00:18:05,970
which went from not existing,

419
00:18:05,970 --> 00:18:10,110
to now being the most popular one on campus.

420
00:18:10,110 --> 00:18:13,080
We had 2000 students graduating.

421
00:18:13,080 --> 00:18:15,750
If you've ever gone to a concert at the Greek Theater,

422
00:18:15,750 --> 00:18:19,500
you know that filling that Greek Theater is a lot of people.

423
00:18:19,500 --> 00:18:21,902
We had to do it twice to fill all

424
00:18:21,902 --> 00:18:25,440
of our undergraduate data science students.

425
00:18:25,440 --> 00:18:28,800
And so you can imagine, just even selfishly,

426
00:18:28,800 --> 00:18:31,800
if some portion of those students

427
00:18:31,800 --> 00:18:35,400
went into careers in public interest technology,

428
00:18:35,400 --> 00:18:37,590
went into public service,

429
00:18:37,590 --> 00:18:38,970
whether at the state government,

430
00:18:38,970 --> 00:18:42,570
local government level, or other options,

431
00:18:42,570 --> 00:18:46,470
imagine what the cascading positive impacts could be.

432
00:18:46,470 --> 00:18:51,003
So I love the theme of trying to, you know,

433
00:18:52,086 --> 00:18:55,140
I wrote this down, reshape tech from the inside.

434
00:18:55,140 --> 00:18:57,870
It actually echoes when we were getting

435
00:18:57,870 --> 00:19:00,150
